<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sessho-and-kampaku">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sesshō and Kampaku</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sekkan, Sesshō, Kampaku, Taikō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sesshō and Kampaku were the two regencies of the Japanese court, together called sekkan: the sesshō acted for a child emperor or an empress regnant, the kampaku assisted an adult emperor as chief adviser and regent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yoshifusa">Fujiwara no Yoshifusa</a> became the first sesshō from outside the imperial house in 858, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a> the first kampaku in 887. Marrying their daughters to emperors, the Fujiwara monopolised both offices from the 10th century and dominated the Heian court, until the cloistered rule of retired emperors from the 11th century left the titles without real power. Held in rotation by the five regent houses, they were abolished in 1868.</p></div>
